The Q4_K superblock scale decode reads all twelve scale bytes as three four-byte-aligned
words and gathers the halfword pair from byte lanes, in place of three loads at two-byte
alignment. block_q4_K holds scales[12] as bytes; the shader reads scales[v_im],
scales[v_im+2], and scales[v_im+4] through the packed16 view, so each is a 16-bit access at
alignment 2, and nir_lower_mem_access_bit_sizes lowers the vectorized first pair to a
dword-aligned buffer_load_dwordx2 plus one alignbyte_amd per component
(shift_load_data_alignbyte_amd), which ACO selects as two v_alignbyte_b32 and a
v_mov_b32_sdwa merge, over an address the body adds up per output row. The packed32 view
carries the same twelve bytes as scales[0..2], four-byte aligned, so one read serves the
pair and the eighth-scale halfword alike and the alignbyte lowering never runs.

The merge is a byte gather rather than a shift-or because the four bytes wanted are lanes
2*v_im and 2*v_im+1 of two words, which is a byte permute; v_im is invariant across both
loops, so compute_outputs forms the selector once and passes it in. The selected bytes are
the bytes the shift-or selected, over the whole twelve-byte input space and both values of
v_im, so the accumulated value is unchanged bit for bit.

Measured on the RADV RAVEN2 shim at the served shape, spec constants 0:64 1:4 2:1: the
superblock loop body falls 395 to 365 instructions and 24 to 16 vector-memory instructions,
its longest dependent chain 17 to 12, and the shader's VGPR allocation 64 to 48, which the
driver reports as 5 subgroups per SIMD against 4. evidence/q4k-scale-decode/ carries the
receipts, the four formulations this one was chosen over, and the appliance arms that
remain unrun.

